{
  "assessment": "The source packet provides a detailed derivation and numerical evidence for the subexponential decay of local correlations in U(1)-symmetric random unitary circuits and translationally invariant Floquet systems. The selected quantitative cluster focuses on the stretched exponential decay of the non-hydrodynamic correlation function in translationally invariant Floquet systems, with a stretch exponent alpha = 2/3. The source reports fitted stretch exponents for three models (A, B, C) at infinite temperature, and also provides a low-density exponential decay regime. The extraction captures the main quantitative claims, mechanisms, controls, readouts, and fixed conditions, with appropriate anchors and provenance. The case is authoring-ready as it includes numeric controls (time, density), numeric readouts (correlation function, stretch exponent), quantitative mechanisms, and source-grounded responses.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Ewan McCulloch, Subexponential Decay of Local Correlations from Diffusion-Limited Dephasing, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/393g-z21y",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/393g-z21y",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2504.05380v2"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper studies the decay of local correlation functions in chaotic quantum systems with conservation laws in one dimension, focusing on non-hydrodynamic operators (e.g., spin raising/lowering operators) that are orthogonal to hydrodynamic modes. It argues that such correlations decay as stretched exponentials or slower, with a stretch exponent of 1/2 for random charge-conserving circuits and 2/3 for translationally invariant Floquet systems. The supplemental material provides derivations, numerical simulations (TEBD, Monte Carlo), and additional models.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "void_dephasing_mechanism",
      "statement": "Local correlations decay subexponentially because rare low-entropy 'voids' (regions close to the vacuum) protect inserted quantum superpositions from dephasing until the void fills in via hydrodynamic processes like diffusion. The decay rate is limited by the void filling time.",
      "equations": [
        "C(t) >= exp(-O(t^{2/3})) for translationally invariant Floquet systems",
        "Z(0,t) >= 2^{-O(t^{1/2+epsilon})} for random circuits"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["C(t)", "Z(0,t)", "t", "ell", "n(x,t)", "alpha"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "stretch_exponent_alpha",
          "value": 0.6666666666666666,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Stretch exponent in the stretched exponential decay exp(-O(t^alpha))",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 590,
              "line_end": 590,
              "support": "C(t) >= exp(-O(t^{2/3}))"
            },
            {
              "line_start": 603,
              "line_end": 603,
              "support": "stretched exponential decay in excellent agreement with our conjectured bound on the stretching exponent alpha=2/3"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 512,
          "line_end": 512,
          "support": "A local quantum superposition inserted into a void does not dephase until the void fills in. Thus the rate at which a void fills in, through hydrodynamic processes like diffusion, limits the late-time decay of local correlation functions."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 515,
          "line_end": 515,
          "support": "if it instead diverges (as in translation invariant Hamiltonian or Floquet systems), the void fills in faster and local correlations decay as exp(-t^{2/3})"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "density_dependent_diffusion",
      "statement": "In translationally invariant Floquet systems, the diffusion constant diverges at low density as D(n) ~ 1/n, leading to a nonlinear diffusion equation for the background density. This controls the void filling dynamics and the resulting stretched exponential decay.",
      "equations": [
        "partial_t n = D_0 partial_x (n^{-1} partial_x n)",
        "n(x,t) ~ t/x^2 in the diffusive scaling regime"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["n(x,t)", "D(n)", "D_0", "eta", "ell"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "diffusion_constant_prefactor_D0",
          "value": 1,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Prefactor in D(n) = D_0/n; set to 1 for simplicity",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 601,
              "line_end": 601,
              "support": "Setting D_0 = 1 for simplicity"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 596,
          "line_end": 598,
          "support": "the density dynamics can therefore be described by a diffusion equation with a density-dependent diffusion constant D(n) ≈ D_0/n. We use these properties to write the phenomenological transport equation: ∂_t n = D_0 ∂_x (n^{-1} ∂_x n)"
        },
        {
          "line_start": 128,
          "line_end": 128,
          "support": "we consider a diffusive fluid with diffusivity D(n) ~ 1/n. This yields the equation ∂_t n = ∂_x (n^{-1} ∂_x n)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
